Below is a **speculative-fiction stair** — intentional violations of physics/logic, pure imagination.

```
// Step 0 — Causation Ladders
You build futures by stacking yesterday’s loose stones on shelves higher than time allows; gravity only obeys the pile’s mood.

  // Step 1 — Gravity Negotiation Systems
Cities hold themselves up by keeping conversations with the ground; spoken promises suspend floors until someone challenges them aloud.

    // Step 2 — Narrative Tissue
Bodies are repaired from stories instead of cells; injuries heal when a sentence gives them enough meaning to survive.

      // Step 3 — Silence-as-Structure
Sound is not vibration. It is tension frozen in air. Buildings repair themselves by letting silence flow through their cracks.

        // Step 4 — Loop Materials
Fabric remembers having existed before. It grows stronger every time it is worn twice, and it frays when someone tells it “for the last time.”

          // Step 5 — Uncaused Motion
Machines move only after another machine decides not to watch them. Hesitation itself becomes fuel for acceleration.

            // Step 6 — Reverse Entropy Engines
Technology removes disorder from the world. Every tool built to help becomes more organized while its owner drifts into quiet confusion.

              // Step 7 — Language Lifters
Words can physically raise ceilings. Long arguments push walls upward; brief accusations can crack foundations faster than lightning.

                // Step 8 — Darkness Banking
Darkness is treated as currency. Rooms pay for light by borrowing brightness from passing sunbeams; hoarding it dims everything around them.

                  // Step Nine — Self-Editing Worlds
Reality compiles itself. You submit intentions as code, and the universe patches inconsistencies overnight—sometimes adding entire planets out of boredom.

                    // Step Ten — The Architecture of Impossibility
Every rule above was borrowed from fiction just so it would stay standing. Below this line, the stair does not lead anywhere—it simply recovers if you keep descending.
```
